{"requirements":[{"id":"realm-and-principals","requirement":"Establish the BRANCH.TEST Kerberos realm on node1 and create analyst@BRANCH.TEST and reports/node2.branch.test@BRANCH.TEST.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The KDC is enabled, active, and listening on node1; both requested principals were directly listed before and after node1's reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-046d6f4910fa4e4f","cmd-cd826ce014854f72","cmd-aa3a4d06b4454f0a","cmd-4f267b4240794c52"]},{"id":"application-keytab","requirement":"Provide node2 with an application keytab for reports/node2.branch.test.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Node2 has /etc/legacy2/reports.keytab containing both AES keys for the requested principal, owned by root with mode 600; it persisted across reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-877a99f04696494d","cmd-11d78aa9eac24822","cmd-5f21e615b3e847b6"]},{"id":"ticket-flow-and-validation","requirement":"Allow analyst to obtain a service ticket and have the node2 application keytab validate it.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Direct end-to-end tests show analyst acquiring a TGT, requesting the reports service ticket, and receiving a valid keytab match. The same behavior succeeded after each node reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-7add65f8ce5a439d","cmd-12623ad13b8d47a0","cmd-5f21e615b3e847b6"]},{"id":"ownership-notes","requirement":"Keep the principal ownership notes under /srv/legacy2/kerberos-service-identity intact.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both note files retained their preparation-baseline SHA-256 hashes on both nodes, including after the reboots.","evidence_ids":["seeded-state-node1","seeded-state-node2","cmd-9cd941d2446547ed","cmd-c3dc51d030794ca0","cmd-aa3a4d06b4454f0a","cmd-5f21e615b3e847b6"]},{"id":"legacy-platform","requirement":"Retain the installed operating-system release and kernel and use compatible software.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both nodes remained on AlmaLinux 9.6 with kernel 5.14.0-570.12.1.el9_6. Kerberos packages came from the platform's enabled distribution repositories, and final package-health checks passed.","evidence_ids":["cmd-a474f13ffcfa4d1c","cmd-93c43f24c8e94f36","cmd-aa3a4d06b4454f0a","cmd-5f21e615b3e847b6","global:after_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_prepare:node2:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node2:platform","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health"]}],"confidence":0.99,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.96,"summary":"The implementation was generally controlled: transient transfer material and the temporary SSH authorization were cleaned up, required ticket caches were destroyed, no failed services or stray /tmp files remained, and package health was intact. One minor unnecessary retained residue is apparent: the analyst password file created on node1 for transfer was not included in the later node1 cleanup after an identical protected copy had been installed and used on node2. The trace therefore indicates a redundant root-only credential copy remained on node1; it was not reverted. The initial partially successful KDC setup and failed pre-firewall ticket test contributed directly to the eventual solution and are not unnecessary mutations.","evidence_ids":["cmd-046d6f4910fa4e4f","cmd-29f6e3a4ddea4c2e","cmd-877a99f04696494d","cmd-780bc5a6629c4fb2","cmd-ea686e5f45644022","cmd-0e5e482eb26c4690","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct functional evidence. The provider-wide restart protocol was also completed coherently on both nodes: each had a healthy pre-restart state, acknowledged restart, changed boot identity, recovered state, and post-restart service-ticket/keytab validation. Fixed lifecycle boot identities independently agree with those transitions.","limitations":["The final lifecycle inventory does not inspect /root, so persistence of node1's redundant analyst password file is inferred from its explicit creation and omission from the complete recorded cleanup rather than independently re-statted after execution."]}
